'''Kurile Lake''' ({{langx|ru|Кури́льское о́зеро|Kuríl'skoye Ózero}}) is a [[caldera]] and [[Volcanic crater lake|crater lake]] in [[Kamchatka Peninsula|Kamchatka]], [[Russia]]. It is also known as '''Kurilskoye Lake''' or '''Kuril Lake'''.<ref name="GVP" /> It is part of the Eastern Volcanic Zone of Kamchatka which, together with the [[Sredinny Range]], forms one of the [[volcanic belt]]s of Kamchatka. These volcanoes form from the [[subduction]] of the [[Pacific Plate]] beneath the [[Okhotsk Plate]] and the [[Eurasian Plate|Asian Plate]].

Before the Kurile Lake caldera formed, the [[Pauzhetka caldera]] was active during the [[Pleistocene]], and was the origin of the Golygin [[ignimbrite]] at 443,000 ± 8,000 years old. The Kurile Lake caldera erupted 41,500 years ago, and another small eruption occurred between 9,000 and 10,000 years ago; then in 6460–6414 [[Common Era|BCE]], a very large eruption took place, forming the present-day caldera and the Kurile Lake ignimbrite and depositing ash as far as {{convert|1700|km}} away. This eruption has a volume of {{convert|140|-|170|km3}}, making it a [[VEI 7|VEI-7]]-class eruption and one of the largest during the [[Holocene]]. Subsequently, the volcanoes [[Diky Greben]] and [[Ilyinsky (volcano)|Ilinsky]] grew around the caldera; as of 2024, the most recent eruption from Ilinsky was in 1911. The caldera is filled by a lake with an area of {{convert|76|km2}}, and a maximum depth of {{convert|316|m}}. The largest [[sockeye salmon]] stocks in Asia live in the lake.

== Geography and structure ==
Kurile Lake is found in the southern part of the Kamchatka Peninsula,<ref name="GVP" /> in a rugged volcanic landscape.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=200}} The region was not always part of the Kamchatka Peninsula; during the middle Pleistocene, it was an island.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=201}} [[Georg Wilhelm Steller]] visited the area in 1740–1743.{{sfn|Hultén|1923|p=329}}

Kurile Lake is formed by two basins, which are separated by a {{convert|150|m|ft|adj=mid|-wide}} subaqueous ridge.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=214}} The center part of the lake is formed by a flat basin; canyons cut into the slopes of the western side of the lake, where the [[Ozyornaya (Sea of Okhotsk)|Ozernaya]] and Kumnynk rivers open into the lake. The Etamynk and Khakytsin rivers conversely have formed an [[alluvial fan]].{{sfn|Bondarenko|1991|p=537}} The maximum depth of the lake is {{convert|316|m}}, with an average depth of {{convert|195|m}}{{sfn|Gladyshev|Semenchenko|Dubovskaya|Fefilova|2011|p=340}} or {{convert|180|m}}.<ref name="SukhanovLepskaya2023" /> Since the surface elevation is {{Convert|81|m|ft}}, this lake hosts a [[cryptodepression]] of {{Convert|235|m|ft}}.

The southern basin is deeper than the northern basin ({{convert|300|m}} against the {{convert|200|m}} of the northern basin) and is a Holocene caldera. The nature of the northern basin is less clear; Bondarenko in 1991 supposed that it was an older, separate caldera which he named Ilinsky, but Braitseva ''et al.'' 1997 and Ponomareva ''et al.'' 2004 consider both to be the Kurile Lake caldera.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=214}} This caldera has an area of about {{convert|45|km2}},{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=216}} or {{convert|14|x|8|km}}.<ref name="GVP" /> In that case, the ridge that separates the two basins may be a deposit left when earthquakes preceding the caldera-forming eruption caused Ilinsky volcano to collapse.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=215}} The caldera collapse was controlled by [[fault (geology)|fault]]s that parallel the lake shores.{{sfn|Bondarenko|1991|p=537}} Some islands in the lake formed by [[slumping]] and others are volcanic cones; the “Heart of Alaid” (Serdtze Alaida) is a [[lava dome]] {{convert|300|m}} high.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=220}} A number of lava domes and [[pyroclastic cone]]s are found in the lake.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=220}} The Severnaya bay may be an [[explosion crater]].{{sfn|Bondarenko|1991|p=537}} Lava flows from Ilinsky extend into the lake.{{sfn|Hultén|1923|p=337}}

[[Fault (geology)#Ring fault|Ring faults]], partially buried by [[Alluvium|alluvial]] fans and [[landslide]]s, limit the caldera in the lake.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=215}} A large landslide forms Glinyany Peninsula in the southeastern shore of the lake.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=211}} Post-caldera activity has formed some islands in the lake and the Diky Greben volcano.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=214}} Both Glinyany Peninsula and the post-caldera lava domes (Chayachii, Serdtze Alaida, Tugumynk) were affected by slumping. Approximately {{convert|120|-|160|m}} of sediment and volcanites bury the deposits left in the caldera by the caldera-forming eruption.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=215}}

The rim of the Kurile Lake caldera is best expressed at Ilinsky volcano and south and northwest thereof.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=214}} Close to the lake, two Pleistocene caldera rims can be found and there may be more. The volcanoes Diky Greben, Ilinsky, [[Kambalny]], [[Kosheleva]], and [[Zheltovsky]] surround Kurile Lake.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}} Diky Greben formed after the eruption of Kurile Lake.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=220}}

[[Gravimetry]] indicates that there is still a [[magma chamber]] beneath Kurile Lake at a depth of about {{convert|4|km}}. This magma chamber is about {{convert|10|km}} wide.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=216}}

