== Geology ==
The Pacific Plate subducts at a pace of about {{convert|8|cm/yr}} beneath the Okhotsk Plate and [[Asian Plate]]. This subduction is responsible for the [[Kuril–Kamchatka Trench|Kamchatka-Kuril Trench]] as well as for volcanism in Kamchatka. The [[Wadati-Benioff Zone]] lies about {{convert|100|km}} beneath Kurile Lake{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=201}} and there is a [[slab window]] under the volcano that might be responsible for its activity.<ref name="ToyokuniZhao2025" />

Kurile Lake is included in the Eastern Volcanic Zone of Kamchatka, {{convert|200|km}} away from the trench.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=201}} It is one of the two or three volcanic zones of Kamchatka, the other ones being the Central Depression and the [[Sredinny Range]]. Only the first two have had historical activity.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Melekestsev|Braitseva|2013|p=168}} The neighbouring volcano Ilinsky was active in 1911, and Zheltovsky was active in 1923.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}} [[Hydrothermal]] activity may still be ongoing in the lake.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=216}}

The oldest volcanoes of the Pauzhetka structure may be of [[Oligocene]]-[[Miocene]] age; during that time, the area was in the sea. The sedimentary Paratunka Formation and the Kurilsky complex were deposited during that time; they are exposed east and southwest of Kurile Lake. About {{convert|600|-|650|km3}} of basaltic rocks were erupted during the Miocene-[[Pliocene]]. The Pauzhetka depression formed during the Pliocene or Pleistocene and was most likely accompanied by the eruption of the {{convert|300|-|450|km3|adj=on}} Golygino [[ignimbrite]].{{sfn|Bondarenko|1991|p=536}} The eruption of this ignimbrite occurred 443,000 ± 8000 years ago.<ref name="Bindeman2010" /> Afterwards, a [[resurgent dome]] named the Kambalny ridge formed in the Pauzhetka structure, as well as a proto-Ilinsky volcano.{{sfn|Bondarenko|1991|p=536}}
[[File:Kurile lake in Southern Kamchatka peninsula map.jpg|thumb|Position of Kurile Lake in southern Kamchatka]]

=== Local ===
The basement in the area is formed by Miocene-Pliocene [[sedimentary rocks]] and [[volcanic rocks]]. During the Pleistocene, calderas, [[lava plateau]]s, and [[somma volcano]]es like pre-Ilinsky [[stratovolcano]]es formed in the area.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}}

One caldera that formed in the area is the [[Pauzhetka Caldera]], of middle Pleistocene age. {{As of|2004|}}, the possibility that a later caldera existed in the Pauzhetka caldera was considered.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}} Kurile Lake is located in the eastern part of this Pauzhetka caldera, which has dimensions of {{convert|55|x|35|km}}. In the center of the Pauzhetka caldera lies a {{convert|650|m|adj=on}}-wide depression that covers an area of {{convert|25|x|20|km}}.{{sfn|Bondarenko|1991|p=533}}

=== Regional explosive activity ===
Southern Kamchatka has been the site of [[explosive eruption]]s during history;{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=201}} the [[Ksudach]] volcano {{convert|50|km}} north of Kurile Lake had five caldera forming eruptions during the Pleistocene and Holocene. The time period between 6400 and 6600 BCE was especially active, with caldera-forming eruptions including the one that formed Kurile Lake.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}}

Kurile Lake is not the only volcano in Kamchatka with major explosive eruptions during the Holocene that exceeded [[VEI 5]]; three other such eruptions occurred at [[Ksudach]] volcano and one at [[Karymsky (volcano)|Karymsky]] volcano.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=200}}

=== Composition ===
Volcanic rocks of Kurile Lake range from [[basaltic andesite]] to [[rhyolite]]. They contain small-to-medium amounts of [[potassium]].{{sfn|Ponomareva|Melekestsev|Braitseva|2013|p=172}}

Rhyolite makes up the bulk of the eruption products of Kurile Lake. Minerals contained include [[plagioclase]], [[orthopyroxene]], [[clinopyroxene]], [[magnetite]], and [[hornblende]], in decreasing order of importance.{{sfn|Braitseva|Melekestsev|Ponomareva|Kirianov|1992|p=177}} The ash becomes white away from the vent, while near-vent deposits are often yellow.{{sfn|Braitseva|Melekestsev|Ponomareva|Kirianov|1992|p=179}}

== Environment ==
[[File:Вечерняя прогулка близ озера. Вид с тропы.jpg|thumb|The environment at Kurile Lake]]

Vegetation around the caldera consists mostly of [[bush (plant)|bush]] and [[forest]].{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=200}} At the edge of the lake there is no macrovegetation.<ref name="TimmVvedenskaya2006" />

Vegetation in Kamchatka overall consists mostly of [[alder]] bushes, [[pine]], and [[Betula ermanii|stone birch]]. Along valleys, [[Populus|cottonwood]] and [[willow]] can be found as well.{{sfn|Revenko|1998|p=11}} In 1998, Kurile Lake had the highest density of [[Kamchatka brown bear|brown bears of Kamchatka]], and possibly of the entirety of Russia.{{sfn|Revenko|1998|p=14}} The lake is a [[nature reserve]].{{sfn|Filatov|Gronskaya|2012|p=678}}

=== Lake ===
[[File:Ozernaya river.jpg|thumb|The Ozernaya river drains the lake.]]

The Kurile Lake caldera is filled by the Kurile crater lake, covering a surface area of {{convert|76|km2}}{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=200}} to {{convert|77.1|km2}}.<ref name="SukhanovLepskaya2023" /> A lake existed already before the Kurile Lake caldera-forming eruption.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}} The present-day lake has a volume of {{convert|14.6|km3}} and a [[catchment]] of {{convert|392|km2}}{{sfn|Milovskaya|Selifonov|Sinyakov|1998|p=434}} and is surrounded by steep shores.{{sfn|Hultén|1923|p=339}} Water remains in the lake for about eighteen years.<ref name="SukhanovLepskaya2023" />

In June 2011, a water temperature of {{convert|1.9|C}} was measured.{{sfn|Gladyshev|Semenchenko|Dubovskaya|Fefilova|2011|p=341}} The lake waters are [[oligotrophic]].<ref name="Lepskaya2010" /> The [[Ozernaya (river, Sea of Okhotsk)|Ozernaya River]] drains the lake to the [[Sea of Okhotsk]].{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=200}} Reports in 1923 indicate that the lake was formerly up to {{convert|50|m}} higher than today, possibly because [[lava flow]]s dammed its outlet. At least two other shorelines are found {{convert|15|-|20|m}} above the current water level.{{sfn|Hultén|1923|p=338}} The caldera lake may have suffered a catastrophic outburst flood in the past.<ref name="Manville2010" />

[[Diatom]]s form most of the [[phytoplankton]], with ''[[Cyclotella]]'', ''[[Melosira]]'', ''[[Stephanodiskus]]'', and ''[[Synedra]]''.{{sfn|Milovskaya|Selifonov|Sinyakov|1998|p=434}} Dominant [[copepod]] species in summer 2011 include ''[[Cyclops scutifer]]'' and the dominant [[cladocera]]ns ''[[Daphnia longiremis]]''.{{sfn|Gladyshev|Semenchenko|Dubovskaya|Fefilova|2011|p=343}} Other species as well as [[rotifer]]s are also present; they constitute sources of food for sockeye salmons.{{sfn|Milovskaya|Selifonov|Sinyakov|1998|p=435}} A number of [[annelid]] species are also found, many of the species that are found in neighboring waters cannot be found in the lake.<ref name="TimmVvedenskaya2006" /> The [[chironomid]] [[midge]] ''[[Chaetocladius tatianae]]'' is endemic to the Kurile Lake watershed.<ref name="MakarchenkoMakarchenko2006" />

Sockeye salmon fisheries are present at the lake.<ref name="Lepskaya2010" /> The lake is a major nursery for this species of fish.{{sfn|Milovskaya|Selifonov|Sinyakov|1998|p=434}} The number of fish in the lake ranges from 260,000 to over 6 million.{{sfn|Milovskaya|Selifonov|Sinyakov|1998|p=435}} The stocks found at Kurile Lake are the largest in [[Asia]].{{sfn|Filatov|Gronskaya|2012|p=678}} Fishing of the salmon of the Ozernaya River has been regulated to allow their reproduction and to conserve [[bear]] populations.{{sfn|Armstrong|Gaffney|2020|p=205}} The lake is part of the Kamchatka National Reserve.{{sfn|Armstrong|Gaffney|2020|p=202}}

== Eruptive history ==
[[File:Kuthiny Baty.jpg|thumb|[[Outcrop]] of pumice at Kuthiny Baty, {{convert|4|km|mile|abbr=on}} from the lake]]

The idea that [[pumice]] around Kurile Lake was formed by an eruption in the area of the lake was first suggested by [[Boris Piip]] in 1947. Later research identified this pumice as the product of the caldera-forming eruption, although some skepticism remains, which considers this pumice as the product of [[Fissure vent|fissure eruptions]].{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}} An earlier Pleistocene caldera-forming eruption took place 41,500 years ago;<ref name="GVP" /> ash deposits from this eruption are found as far away as [[Magadan]], {{convert|1000|km}} away from Kurile Lake,<ref name="Braitseva1995" /> and possibly [[Lake El'gygytgyn|Lake El′gygytgyn]].<ref name="Juschus2009" />

The Kurile Lake caldera forming eruption, also known as "KO",{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}} occurred in 6460–6414 [[Common Era|BCE]].{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=218}} It is the largest known Holocene eruption in Kamchatka. [[Tephra]] from this eruption has been found in southern Kamchatka and also Magadan in Asia.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}} The total volume of the Kurile Lake caldera forming eruption is about {{convert|140|-|170|km3}}, corresponding to a [[Volcanic Explosivity Index|volcanic explosivity index]] of 7 and comparable to the [[1815 eruption of Mount Tambora|1815 eruption of Tambora]].<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/gsa/geology/article-abstract/12/11/659/188320/Volcanological-study-of-the-great-Tambora-eruption?redirectedFrom=PDF|doi = 10.1130/0091-7613(1984)12<659:VSOTGT>2.0.CO;2|title = Volcanological study of the great Tambora eruption of 1815|year = 1984|last1 = Self|first1 = S.|last2 = Rampino|first2 = M. R.|last3 = Newton|first3 = M. S.|last4 = Wolff|first4 = J. A.|journal = Geology|volume = 12|issue = 11|page = 659|bibcode = 1984Geo....12..659S|url-access = subscription}}</ref><ref>[http://www.kscnet.ru/ivs/bibl/vulk/kuozero/Pon-KurileLake.pdf The 7600 (14C) year BP Kurile Lake caldera-forming eruption ... - CORE]</ref> Other volcanoes with such large eruptions during the Holocene include [[Baitoushan]], [[Crater Lake]] and [[Kikai Caldera|Kikai]].{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=217}}

The caldera forming eruption commenced with a [[Phreatomagmatic eruption|phreatoplinian]] eruption that generated deposits of fine ash. Several yellowish [[rhyolitic]] ignimbrites were erupted, reaching a thickness of over {{convert|50|m}}. These ignimbrites filled gullies around the lake and also reach thicknesses of several tens of centimeters in the [[Vychenkiya River]] and [[Unkanovich River]] valleys.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=207}} This eruption phase occurred through the lake.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=218}} All these deposits were formed by the same event. Subsequently, a short eruption of [[lapilli]] and pumice consisting of [[dacite]] and rhyolite occurred, most of it falling towards the northwest; their thickness reaches {{convert|20|cm}} north of the lake. Some [[basaltic]] [[scoria]] was also deposited during this phase.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=208}} At this point, the [[Volcano|vent]] had emerged above the water level and was generating an [[eruption column]] that deposited ash over southern Kamchatka. Eventually, the vent widened and caused the eruption column to collapse. At this point, [[pyroclastic flow]]s formed and deposited the Kurile Lake ignimbrite.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=218}} Reaching a thickness of {{convert|150|m}} close to the lake, it filled valleys, overran plateaus and ridges, and reached both the [[Pacific Ocean]] and the [[Sea of Okhotsk]].{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=208}} The ignimbrite was highly mobile, overrunning high topographic obstacles and flowing along valleys in a complex flow pattern.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|pp=209–211}} The ignimbrite covered a total surface area of {{convert|1800|-|1900|km2}}.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=216}} This ignimbrite consists of rocks ranging from basaltic andesite to rhyolite with colors ranging from white to dark. Unusually for such mixed-composition ignimbrites, the rhyolites overlie the more [[mafic]] deposits.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=208}} These mafic ignimbrites are not found around the entire lake, indicating that the magma chamber was asymmetric or its contents were erupted in an asymmetric fashion.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=218}} The ignimbrite contains remnants of vegetation, accretionary structures formed when the ignimbrite interacted with water, and [[breccia]] probably formed when conditions at the vent changed,{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=209}} involving the formation of a ring vent.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=218}} [[Fumarole]]s formed as the ignimbrite overran rivers.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=219}} Some post-eruption alteration of the ignimbrite deposits also took place.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=211}} In the lake itself, the ignimbrite is about {{convert|400|m}} thick.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=215}} The pumice deposits have been affected by [[erosion]] and possibly by [[fumarolic]] activity, forming structures resembling overturned boats that were named “Khutk's boats” by native settlers.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}}

Ash from the eruption spread west-northwest of Kurile Lake,{{sfn|Braitseva|Melekestsev|Ponomareva|Kirianov|1992|p=178}} covering a total surface of over {{convert|2000000|km2}}.{{sfn|Plechov|Balashova|Dirksen|2010|p=974}} It can be found at large distances from the caldera; {{convert|1|mm|in|2|adj=mid|-thick}} layers have been found in the upper reaches of the [[Indigirka River]], {{convert|1700|km}} away from Kurile Lake,{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=211}} and in the [[Oymyakon Plateau]].<ref name="Burnatny2020" /> Thicknesses still reach several centimeters in Magadan. In the northern [[Kurile]]s, the thickness reaches several tens of centimeters. This ash is found in drilling cores in the Sea of Okhotsk.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=211}} Coignimbrite ash formed when the ignimbrites reached the sea.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=219}} In terms of composition, it ranges from rhyolite to dacite and is poor in potassium.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=211}} The ash is an important [[Tephrochronology|tephrochronological]] marker{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|pp=211,214}} and has been found as far away as [[Greenland]].{{sfn|Plechov|Balashova|Dirksen|2010|p=974}}

Before the eruption, a 1,500-year lull in volcanic activity allowed the deposition of soils in the area.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=202}} A minor eruption occurred at Kurile Lake between 9,000 and 10,000 years ago, resulting in the deposition of [[tephra]] north of the caldera. This tephra is formed by gray fine ash and lapilli consisting of [[dacitic]] pumice. Other volcanoes also left several tephra deposits.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=206}} Soils formed after the caldera forming eruptions also contain a number of ash layers by volcanoes both near and far.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=211}} Volcanic activity occurred at [[Ilyinsky (volcano)|Ilinsky]] volcano after the caldera forming eruption until 1901, and the similarity between Ilinsky and Kurile Lake rocks indicate that the activities of the two centers are related.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=219}} Diky Greben formed less than 100 years after the formation of the caldera and was last active 1600 [[Before Present|BP]]. A number of other lava domes and pyroclastic cones formed inside the caldera shortly after the caldera-forming eruption.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=220}}

=== Effects and threats ===

The caldera-forming eruption of Kurile Lake had a devastating effect on the surrounding area and had a noticeable effect far from the lake. A significant amount of gas was released during the eruption, including 3.7–4.2&nbsp;billion metric tons of [[water]], 43–49&nbsp;million tons of [[chlorine]], 8.6–9.8&nbsp;million tons of [[fluorine]], and 26–29&nbsp;million tons of [[sulfur]], comparable to the amounts released by [[Mount Tambora|Tambora]] in 1815 and by [[Huaynaputina]] in 1600.{{sfn|Plechov|Balashova|Dirksen|2010|p=976}} Two [[Sulfate|sulfate spikes]] identified in the [[GISP2]] [[ice core]] of Greenland around 6470 and 6476 BC may be linked to the Kurile Lake eruption.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|pp=217–218}}<ref name="SiglToohey2022" /> The Kurile Lake eruption may have influenced the global [[climate]].{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=219}}

The eruption devastated the vegetation in southern Kamchatka, causing an ecological catastrophe.{{sfn|Ponomareva|Kyle|Melekestsev|Rinkleff|2004|p=219}} Close to Kurile Lake, all vegetation would have been wiped out, and deposits left by the eruption would have hampered the revegetation as well. In more favorable terrain where the volcanic deposits were quickly removed, some plants like ''[[Green alder|Alnus fruticosa]]'' did survive and quickly resettled the terrain.<ref name="DirksenDirksen" />

